# facts.ges — what a document is made of, said in the language.
#
# **A document is facts**: a text of record lines, a line being a kind
# word and its fields, in an agreed order, with a key per kind.  Henri,
# 2026-09-09, `card:gui-is-difficult.md` §"The document, decided" and
# §"The name, and what falls out".  `.notes` and `.desk` were both
# already written this way and neither said so; this is where they say
# it.
#
# **A declaration is a value, the way a chart is a value.**  `chart.ges`
# made that choice first and it is the same reason here: one value is
# executed, printed, and enumerated for checking, and nothing has to be
# added to the language for a document to describe itself.  A file that
# owns a document declares two things — **the model, and how it is
# written**:
#
#     model : List Rel        # the relations: headings, keys, domains
#     lines : List Line       # one line per record, and what it folds in
#
# and what puts them there is an expression in the file, where you can
# read it — `gui.ges`' rule, kept.
#
# **The relations come first and the text is their projection** —
# Henri, 2026-09-14, `card:relational-model.md` §"The logical turn":
# *"se normalisoitujen relaatioiden esitys ehkä kuuluisi olla esillä,
# ja esitysnäkymä tulisi olla sen mukaan, eikä toisinpäin."*  A `Rel`
# is a heading with a key; an `Of` is a relation keyed by another's key
# — the home of a value a record may or may not carry, or a list of
# them with a rank — and a `Line` says which relations one written
# line folds together, in what shape and order.  What `notes.py` reads
# and writes by, the `Kind` below with its `Field`s, is **derived** from
# these by `lineKind` and declared by nobody: `Need`, `Names` and
# `Each` are facts about a line, not about the model, and they fall out
# of where a column lives.
#
# **What this does not decide.**  Nothing here parses or writes; it says
# what the parser and the writer are to be held to.  The first use of it
# is a parity test against `gestate/notes.py`, which has carried these
# facts by hand since the format existed.
#
# **And a program reads its document through one word** — 2026-09-13,
# `card:gui-is-difficult.md` §"The mashup, asked":
#
#     board = document "mark"
#
# the rows of one kind of the file the program `include`s, as a signal
# of a set the host writes at every change of the file: the key fields
# and then each required field, in the declared order, a `Number` an
# `Int` and a `Word` a `Text`.  **Its type is computed from the kind**
# — `kindRow` below, run by the compiler before the program is checked
# (`card:strict-forms.md` §"Decided — 2026-09-14"), so nothing about
# the row is written by hand and a kind edited here changes what the
# checker accepts.  The same row is a type wherever a program wants
# one: `type Row = $(rowType markRel)`.  What the program asks done to
# the file is an `Act` — `gui.ges`' command language: `Assert`,
# `Retract` or `Refuse`, a fact being the kind's word over its columns'
# values as atoms — carried by the element that does it (`Does`) and
# performed by the host on the press; `asserting` and `retracting`
# below build one from the kind and its typed row.
# `examples/gui/tic-tac-toe-facts.ges` is the first program written so.

#: `command.ges` declares this too, and for the same reason: it is not
#: in the core prelude and a file that wants strings has to say so.
type Text = List Char

# ── The model ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#: What a column's value is drawn from — a `Value` below, one of the
#: five a single token can be: `Word`, `Number`, `Range lo hi`,
#: `AtLeast n`, `OneOf names` (`card:relational-model.md` Q2, the
#: domains Codd put in the schema).  `Names` and `Each` are no domain
#: of a column: a list is an `Of` with a rank.
Value := Word | Number | Names | Range Int Int | AtLeast Int | OneOf (List Text) | Each (List Text)

Col := Col Text Value

#: A relation: its name, its heading in order — **the key first**, so
#: a row reads as its identity and then its facts — and its key.
#: **An empty key means at most one row in a document.**
#:
#: `Of parent field cols key need` is a relation keyed by its parent's
#: key, named `parent.field`: with no key of its own it holds at most
#: one row per parent — a value a record *may* carry, absent as no row
#: and never a null — and with a `rank` key it holds a list, one row a
#: name, the order a fact.  `need` says whether every parent must have
#: at least one row there — `Must` for a section's voices, `May` for a
#: note's manners — which is the one flag the model keeps, because it
#: is a rule about the model (an inclusion) and not about a line.
Rel := Rel Text (List Col) (List Text)
     | Of Text Text (List Col) (List Text) Need

#: How one written line is laid out: its kind word, its shape, the
#: fields it folds in — a column of the relation named by the word, or
#: an `Of` of it — in the order they are written, and the order lines
#: of this kind are written in.  A `Headed` shape names the one token
#: written without a field name, and that name is not among the
#: fields; a `Bare` line's one field is.
Line := Line Text Shape (List Text) (List Sort)

# ── A value, and the line view ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# **The line view is derived from the model and the lines, declared by
# nobody.**  It is the form `notes.py` reads a file by and the
# canonical writer writes it by, and the form `grid.ges`' tables are
# functions of; a `Kind` is one `Line` with every field it folds in
# resolved to what it is — a column, a value that may be absent, a list.

#: What one token is.  Every value in this format is one
#: whitespace-free token (`notes.py`, F203), so this says how to read
#: it and what bound it is under.  `Range lo hi` and `AtLeast n` are
#: numbers with a bound; `OneOf names` is a word from a list.
#: one whitespace-free token (`notes.py`, F203), so this says how to
#: read that token and nothing about layout.
#:
#: `Names` is the comma-separated list `voices melody,upper,bass`
#: already writes — one token still, and the only shape beyond a word
#: and a number that the two formats in the tree use.
#:
#: `Names` and `Each names` are how an `Of` with a rank reads on the
#: line — one comma-joined token, each name from a list or not — and
#: what a reader does with a bound is derive the refusal from it.

#: Whether a record of this kind has to carry the field.  The refusal a
#: missing one earns names it, so this is what makes that message
#: derivable rather than written twice.
Need := Must | May

Field := Field Text Value Need

# ── A kind ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#: What the tokens after the kind word are.
#:
#: `Named` — `key value key value …`, and nothing positional, which is
#: gate two of `spec/drawnscores.md`: *two fields of one shape telling
#: apart only by position* is the failure that made `manner`
#: unfindable.  `note` is this.
#:
#: `Bare` — one value and no name, under the field name given.  `bpm 96`
#: is this, and so is every singleton line of a `.desk`.
#:
#: `Headed` — one bare value first, then named fields.  `section A  key
#: D  bars 8` is this, and the bare one is the record's own name.
Shape := Named | Bare Text | Headed Text

#: How the lines of a kind are sorted when the file is written back.
#:
#: `By` is the ordinary case: this field, ascending.
#:
#: `Along` is the one the roll forced — the voice order is *the
#: section's own*, not alphabetical, because that is the order the roll
#: stacks them in (Henri, 2026-09-05).  So it names the field, the kind
#: that writes the order down, and that kind's field holding it.
#:
#: `Among` is where a reference is ordered by **where the record it
#: names stands**: a note's `section` sorts by the section's place in
#: the file and not by its name, because a section is put where the
#: author put it.  Found 2026-09-09 by deriving the writer from this
#: declaration and noticing that `By "section"` agreed with the parser
#: only because `arc.notes` happens to name its sections A, B, C.
Sort := By Text | Along Text Text Text | Among Text Text

#: One kind of line, resolved: its word, the shape of its line, its
#: fields, the key of the relation it writes, and the order its lines
#: are written in — `lineKind` builds one from a `Line` and the model.
#:
#: **An empty order means the order they were written in**, which is
#: what `section` has: a section list is the author's sequence and
#: sorting it would be a second thing to be canonical about.
Kind := Kind Text Shape (List Field) (List Text) (List Sort)

# ── Reading one ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Plain projections, so a host reads a kind without knowing the
# constructor's shape — `chart.ges`' `initial`/`advance` are the same
# courtesy.

kindName : Kind -> Text
kindName k = case k of
    Kind n s fs ks os -> n

kindShape : Kind -> Shape
kindShape k = case k of
    Kind n s fs ks os -> s

kindFields : Kind -> List Field
kindFields k = case k of
    Kind n s fs ks os -> fs

kindKey : Kind -> List Text
kindKey k = case k of
    Kind n s fs ks os -> ks

kindOrder : Kind -> List Sort
kindOrder k = case k of
    Kind n s fs ks os -> os

fieldName : Field -> Text
fieldName f = case f of
    Field n v d -> n

#: The fields a record of this kind must carry — the list a refusal
#: names, derived rather than written a second time.
required : Kind -> List Text
required k = map fieldName (filter mustHave (kindFields k))

mustHave : Field -> Bool
mustHave f = case f of
    Field n v d -> case d of
        Must -> True
        May -> False

#: The kind of this name, if the document has one.
kindOf : List Kind -> Text -> List Kind
kindOf ks name = filter (k => kindName k == name) ks

# ── Reading the model ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#: A relation's name — `parent.field` for an `Of`.
relName : Rel -> Text
relName r = case r of
    Rel n cs k -> n
    Of p f cs k need -> p ++ "." ++ f

#: The relation of this name, if the model has one.
relOf : List Rel -> Text -> List Rel
relOf rs name = filter (r => relName r == name) rs

#: A relation's heading, in order: an `Of`'s is its parent's key
#: columns and then its own.
relCols : List Rel -> Rel -> List Col
relCols rs r = case r of
    Rel n cs k -> cs
    Of p f cs k need -> keyCols rs p ++ cs

#: A relation's key: an `Of`'s is its parent's and then its own.
relKey : List Rel -> Rel -> List Text
relKey rs r = case r of
    Rel n cs k -> k
    Of p f cs k need -> parentKey rs p ++ k

parentKey : List Rel -> Text -> List Text
parentKey rs p = case relOf rs p of
    r :: rest -> relKey rs r
    Nil -> Nil

keyCols : List Rel -> Text -> List Col
keyCols rs p = case relOf rs p of
    r :: rest -> filter (c => elem (colName c) (relKey rs r)) (relCols rs r)
    Nil -> Nil

colName : Col -> Text
colName c = case c of
    Col n d -> n

colDomain : Col -> Value
colDomain c = case c of
    Col n d -> d

colNamed : List Col -> Text -> List Col
colNamed cs n = filter (c => colName c == n) cs

# ── The line view, derived ───────────────────────────────────────────────

#: **A `Kind` from a `Line` and the model** — the derivation `notes.py`
#: and the writer are held to.  Each field the line folds in is a
#: column of the line's relation (`Must`), or an `Of` of it: with no
#: key of its own a value the record may carry (`May`), with a rank a
#: list — `Each` its names when the value column is bounded, `Names`
#: otherwise — required or not as the `Of` says.
#: A field nothing owns yields no `Field` here and is refused by name
#: in the host (`facts.line_kind`), which reads the same model and
#: lines and is held to this.
lineKind : List Rel -> Line -> Kind
lineKind rs l = case l of
    Line w shape fs sort -> Kind w shape (join (map (lineField rs w) fs)) (parentKey rs w) sort

lineField : List Rel -> Text -> Text -> List Field
lineField rs w f = case relOf rs w of
    r :: rest -> case colNamed (relCols rs r) f of
        c :: more -> Field f (colDomain c) Must :: Nil
        Nil -> ofField rs (w ++ "." ++ f) f
    Nil -> Nil

ofField : List Rel -> Text -> Text -> List Field
ofField rs name f = case relOf rs name of
    r :: rest -> case r of
        Of p g cs k need -> case k of
            Nil -> Field f (valueDomain cs) May :: Nil
            ranked -> Field f (listValue (valueDomain cs)) need :: Nil
        Rel n cs k -> Nil
    Nil -> Nil

#: The domain of an `Of`'s `value` column.
valueDomain : List Col -> Value
valueDomain cs = case colNamed cs "value" of
    c :: rest -> colDomain c
    Nil -> Word

#: How a list reads on the line: each name from the list, or names.
listValue : Value -> Value
listValue d = case d of
    OneOf ns -> Each ns
    other -> Names

# ── A type, as a value ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# **The compiler's own type grammar, as a value a program can build** —
# `card:strict-forms.md` Q5, Henri, 2026-09-14: *the wide one, kept
# minimal.*  A `TyCon` is a named type, `TyApp` applies one to another,
# `TyFun` is the arrow, `TyInt` a type-level number such as the `12` of
# `Cyclic 12`, `TyTuple` a tuple.  The compiler knows these five by
# name and nothing else here; what a row *is* stays in this file, in
# the language, where a reader can see it.
#
# A `Type` reaches the checker through one door, the splice: `$(e)`
# in a type position, where `e` is an expression of this type that the
# compiler evaluates before checking the program.  `document "kind"`
# is the same door with `kindRow` already behind it.

Type := TyCon Text | TyApp Type Type | TyFun Type Type | TyInt Int | TyTuple (List Type)

#: What a field's value is, as a type: a number is an `Int`, a word or
#: a bounded word a `Text`.  Names have no base column and no row type.
fieldType : Field -> Type
fieldType f = case f of
    Field n v need -> valueType v

#: A value's type: a number is an `Int`, a word or a bounded word a
#: `Text`, and so are names.
valueType : Value -> Type
valueType v = case v of
    Word -> TyCon "Text"
    Number -> TyCon "Int"
    Names -> TyCon "Text"
    Range lo hi -> TyCon "Int"
    AtLeast lo -> TyCon "Int"
    OneOf ns -> TyCon "Text"
    Each ns -> TyCon "Text"

#: Whether a field is one token a row carries — everything but a list of
#: names, whose column is a relation of its own.
fieldScalar : Field -> Bool
fieldScalar f = case f of
    Field n v need -> case v of
        Names -> False
        Each ns -> False
        Word -> True
        Number -> True
        Range lo hi -> True
        AtLeast lo -> True
        OneOf ns -> True

#: The field named `n`, if the kind has one.
fieldNamed : Kind -> Text -> List Field
fieldNamed k n = filter (f => fieldName f == n) (kindFields k)

#: **The columns of a kind's base relation**, by name: the key, in key
#: order, then every required scalar field not in it, in declared
#: order.  This is the row `document` feeds and the fields a `Fact`
#: carries — `facts.base_columns` in the host, held equal to this by
#: `test/test_stage.py`.  A `Headed` kind's key names its bare first
#: token, which is no field of the record.
kindColumns : Kind -> List Text
kindColumns k = kindKey k ++ map fieldName (filter (f => kindColumn k f) (kindFields k))

kindColumn : Kind -> Field -> Bool
kindColumn k f = case f of
    Field n v need -> case need of
        May -> False
        Must -> case elem n (kindKey k) of
            True -> False
            False -> fieldScalar f

#: A column's type: its field's, or a `Text` for a `Headed` kind's bare
#: name.
columnType : Kind -> Text -> Type
columnType k n = case fieldNamed k n of
    f :: rest -> fieldType f
    Nil -> TyCon "Text"

#: **The row of a kind, as a type**: a tuple of its columns' types — or
#: the one column's type, when there is one.
kindRow : Kind -> Type
kindRow k = case map (columnType k) (kindColumns k) of
    t :: Nil -> t
    ts -> TyTuple ts

#: **The row of a relation, as a type**: a tuple of its heading's
#: types — or the one column's, when there is one.  `rowType` is for a
#: relation that stands alone; `relRow` reads an `Of`'s parent key off
#: the model.
rowType : Rel -> Type
rowType r = relRow (r :: Nil) r

relRow : List Rel -> Rel -> Type
relRow rs r = case map (valueType @ colDomain) (relCols rs r) of
    t :: Nil -> t
    ts -> TyTuple ts

#: The rows of the relation named `name` — what `document "name"` is
#: typed by, `document "section.voices"` included; an empty list is a
#: relation the program does not declare, and the compiler says so on
#: the author's line.
documentRow : List Rel -> Text -> List Type
documentRow rs name = map (relRow rs) (relOf rs name)

# ── A fact, from a kind and its row ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# **The typed door out** — `card:strict-forms.md` Q9, 2026-09-14.  An
# `Act` carries a kind's word and a list of atoms, so that `gui.ges`
# can declare it before any program; what a program *writes* is the
# kind and its row, the same tuple `document` feeds and `kindRow`
# types, and the atoms are derived here.  `Atoms` is the class of a
# row: a number, a word, or a tuple of them.  A program holds the row
# to the relation by naming it once — `type Placing = $(rowType markRel)`
# — and `asserting markRel (k, m)` then reads as the fact it is.

class Atoms r where
    atoms : r -> List Atom

instance Atoms Int where
    atoms n = IntAtom n :: Nil

instance Atoms (List Char) where
    atoms s = TextAtom s :: Nil

instance (Atoms a, Atoms b) => Atoms (a, b) where
    atoms p = case p of
        (a, b) -> atoms a ++ atoms b

instance (Atoms a, Atoms b, Atoms c) => Atoms (a, b, c) where
    atoms p = case p of
        (a, b, c) -> atoms a ++ atoms b ++ atoms c

instance (Atoms a, Atoms b, Atoms c, Atoms d) => Atoms (a, b, c, d) where
    atoms p = case p of
        (a, b, c, d) -> atoms a ++ atoms b ++ atoms c ++ atoms d

instance (Atoms a, Atoms b, Atoms c, Atoms d, Atoms e) => Atoms (a, b, c, d, e) where
    atoms p = case p of
        (a, b, c, d, e) -> atoms a ++ atoms b ++ atoms c ++ atoms d ++ atoms e

#: Assert this row of this kind — `mark  cell 4  mark X` from
#: `asserting markRel (4, "X")`.
asserting : (Atoms r) => Rel -> r -> Act
asserting k r = Assert (relName k) (atoms r)

#: Retract the row with this key — the host matches the key columns and
#: the rest of the row is what the file says.
retracting : (Atoms r) => Rel -> r -> Act
retracting k r = Retract (relName k) (atoms r)
